<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.11.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00562</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.11.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e916eb2f-49ff-4a58-9aa8-299e6fc77fae</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
  <para>For 1 - 3, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the inequality \(y\;\gt\;x − 1.\)</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((0,1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution.
      \(1 &gt; 0 − 1\)
  \(1 &gt; − 1\)
  1 is greater than -1. Yes it is a solution because the inequality is true</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(
      (−4,−1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((−4,−1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(-1 &gt; -4 − 1\)
  \(-1 &gt; −5\)
  -1 is greater than -5. Yes it is a solution because the inequality is true.
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((4,2)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((4, 2)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(2 &gt; 4 − 1\)
  \(2 &gt; 3\)
  2 is not greater than 3. No it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
  
  <para>For 4 - 6, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the inequality \(y\;\gt\;x − 3.\) </para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((0,0)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 0)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(0 &gt; 0 −3\)
  \(0 &gt; -3\)
  0 is greater than -3. Yes it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((2,1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((2, 1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(1 &gt; 2 −3\)
  \(1 &gt; -1\)
  1 is greater than -1. Yes it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((−1,−5)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((-1,-5)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(-5 &gt; -1 −3\)
  \(-5 &gt; -4\)
  -5 is not greater than -4. No it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
  <para>For questions 7 and 8, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the inequality \(2x + 3y &gt; 2.\)</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((1,1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Yes</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((1, 1)\) into the inequality \(2x+3y&gt;2\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(2(1)+3(1) &gt; 2\)
  \(5&gt;2\)
  5 is greater than 2. Yes it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((4,−3)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((4,-3)\) into the inequality \(2x+3y&gt;2\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(2(4)+3(-3) &gt; 2\)
  \(8-9 &gt; 2\)
  \(-1 &gt; 2\)
  -1 is not greater than 2. No it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
    <item>Which inequality represents the graph?</item>
  </list>
  <figure><media alt="graph of y &lt; 3x − 4"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8f554c15c06d4fbb6743789abf840d7e817b0442"/></media></figure><list>
    <item>\( y \leq 3x − 4 \)</item>
    <item>\( y &lt; 3x − 4 \)</item>
    <item> \( y &gt; 3x − 4 \)</item>
    <item>\( y \geq 3x − 4 \)</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \( y &lt; 3x − 4 \)</para>
  <para>The graph has a dotted line, so any answers that have \(\geq\) or\(\leq\) can be eliminated. 
  The two remaining options are \(y&gt;3x−4\) and \(y&gt;3x−4\).
  If we set \(x\) to 0, we can see that the \(y\)-coordinate is -4. Look at the shading in the graph to determine if \(y&gt;-4\) or \(y&gt;-4\). The graph is shaded to the right and down so \(y&gt;-4\). Therefore the equation \(y&gt;3x−4\) is the correct answer.</para>
  
  <para>For questions 10 and 11, use this  graph of the equation \(2y−x=1\) as a guide to answer the questions.</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a line."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9d4d8b641a0b7f9aa831396e126584e15641988e"/></media></figure></para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
    <item>Is the point \( (0,\frac{1}{2}) \) a solutions to the equation?</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes
  </para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((0, \frac{1}{2})\) into the equation  \(2y−x=1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(2(\frac{1}{2})−0=1\)
  \(1-0=1\)
  \(1=1\)
  1 equals 1. Yes it is a solution because the equation is true.
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
    <item>Is the point \((0,2)\) a solution to the inequality \(2y −
      x &gt; 1\)?</item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 2)\) into the inequality \(2y−x&gt;1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(2(2)−0&gt;1\)
  \(4-0&gt;1\)
  \(4&gt;1\)
  4 is greater than 1. Yes it is a solution because the inequality is true.
  </para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
    <item>Consider the linear equation \(2y − 3x = 5\). The pair \((-1,1)\) is a solution to the equation. Which of
      the following is also a solution to the equation?</item>
  </list>
  <list>
    <item>\(
      (1, 5)\) </item>
    <item> \((3, 7)\) </item>
    <item> \((5, 11)\) </item>
    <item> \((-3, -1)\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((3, 7)\)</para>
  <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((3, 7)\) into the equation \(2y−3x=5\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
  \(2(7)−3(3)=5\)
  \(14-9=5\)
  \(5=5\)
  5 equals 5. The ordered pair (3, 7) is a solution because the equation is true.</para>
  
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="13" type="enumerated">
    <item>The boundary line on the graph represents the equation \( 5x + 2y = 6\). Write an inequality that is represented by the graph.</item>
  </list>
  <figure><media alt="graph with boundary line that represents the equation 5x + 2y = 6"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a9a4189f06a5a2df644f610859ad8385a70f7031"/></media></figure><list>
    <item> \(5x + 2y &lt; -6\) </item>
    <item> \(-5x - 2y &lt; 6\) </item>
    <item> \(5x + 2y &lt; 2\) </item>
    <item>\( 5x + 2y &lt; 6\) </item>
  </list>
  <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(5x + 2y &lt; 6 \)</para>
  <para>The graph is shaded to the left and down and it crosses the \(y\)-axis at 3, so the solution has to show the 
  \(y&gt;3\) when \(x=0\).
  If we plug in 0 into the inequality \(5x+2y&gt;6\)
  \(5(0)+2y&gt;6\)
  \(2y&gt;6\)
  \(y&gt;3\)</para>
  
</section>
</content>
